  "worked_solution": "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6}-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1\\sqrt{14}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=1,$ and $a+b=3.$",
